From 3f225938eae9cf6b63806fa0ea2100d779ba1473 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shamik Saha <shamik.saha.rcciit@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:18:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] packaging: declare what the examples need

`examples/langgraph_finance.py` and `examples/langgraph_tools.py` import
langgraph and langchain-core. Nothing declared them, so following the
integration guide produced `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named
'langchain_core'` -- on a clean install of the package the guide is
about.

`pip install "onedoor[examples]"`. They stay out of the runtime
dependencies: onedoor governs LangGraph, it does not require it.

Third undeclared-dependency defect in two days (migrations missing from
the wheel, tzdata on Windows, now this). The pattern is the same each
time: it works in the environment where it was written, and the
declaration that would make it work elsewhere is the thing nobody tests.
---
 docs/integration-langgraph.md | 3 +++
 pyproject.toml                | 5 +++++
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/integration-langgraph.md b/docs/integration-langgraph.md
index 97b99a2..f6d0989 100644
--- a/docs/integration-langgraph.md
+++ b/docs/integration-langgraph.md
@@ -21,6 +21,9 @@ treated as zero, so a euro cap on a wrapped tool silently permitted everything.
 An unresolvable amount is now a denial with reason `cost_unknown`. There is a
 worked example in `examples/langgraph_finance.py`.
 
+The examples need LangGraph, which the engine itself does not:
+`pip install "onedoor[examples]"`.
+
 ## Wrapping tools
 
 ```python
diff --git a/pyproject.toml b/pyproject.toml
index d91fffc..d13833e 100644
--- a/pyproject.toml
+++ b/pyproject.toml
@@ -17,6 +17,11 @@ dependencies = [
 [project.optional-dependencies]
 service = ["fastapi>=0.115", "uvicorn>=0.34"]
 otel = ["opentelemetry-api>=1.27"]
+# Everything under examples/ needs these. They are not runtime
+# dependencies of the engine -- onedoor governs LangGraph, it does not
+# require it -- but the examples are documented artifacts and a reader who
+# follows the docs should not have to guess what to install.
+examples = ["langgraph>=1.0", "langchain-core>=1.0"]
 dev = ["pytest>=8.3", "mypy>=1.14", "ruff>=0.8", "fastapi>=0.115", "httpx>=0.27"]
 
 [tool.pytest.ini_options]
-- 
2.43.0

